# the PE image writer: an executable from the image record of a link
#
# every address, file offset and size of an output section comes from the
# layout record, and every byte from the relocated record. the writer builds
# the PE/COFF model the PE/COFF writer turns into bytes. the headers are the
# MS-DOS header and program every PE linker writes, the PE signature, the file
# header, the optional header with its sixteen data directories and a section
# header for each section of the image, which the header measure sizes from
# the laid out image. the optional header layout is the one whose address is
# as wide as the architecture's, the machine is the architecture's COFF code,
# the image base the host's, the section and file alignment the layout
# rules', the subsystem the target's or else the host's, and the system
# version the target's, else the host's, else the one link.exe states. the
# characteristics follow the layout and the output kind: an image the loader
# may place anywhere is dynamic base, and high entropy where its addresses
# are 64 bits wide. every executable is NX compatible and terminal server
# aware, as link.exe makes one. the checksum is PE Format's, of the bytes
# written.
#
# a section no segment loads, as the debugging information a program database
# would hold, is no section of the image and is left out, as are output
# sections that hold no byte. .reloc is a table the image reserves before
# layout, measured from the places the loader rebases once their addresses
# are known, and laid out last in a segment of its own, as link.exe places
# it: one block per 4 KiB page, in address order, each padded to a multiple of
# four bytes. the writer fills it from the link's loader relocations, which
# take the bytes layout left. an image the loader may place anywhere and that
# holds no address to relocate is refused, as link.exe refuses it. a link that needs what the image does not hold yet, an import,
# an export or thread-local data, is refused naming what it needs.

